  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/a/a4/Strange_Tales_Vol_1_116.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The Puppet Master is back (having survived what appeared to have been certain death at the end of [LINK:1963:FF_14|FF #14|2026-08-06] when his sub was grabbed by a giant octopus), and he thinks he's learned his lesson on how to defeat the FF -- don't battle them all at once -- \"Divide and conquer!\" he says while fashioning a figure of the Human Torch.\n\nHe commands the Torch to go to Alicia's, and once there, Johnny tries to sweep her off her feet!&nbsp; Ben is none too happy to see this when he arrives!&nbsp; And so the fight between the Thing and Torch begins, with the Puppet Master's work apparently done.\n\nAs the battle begins in earnest, furniture flies (and smashes), doors are torn off hinges, etc. before they finally head upstairs to the roof (Johnny getting there by burning a hole through the apartment ceiling).\n\nA few pages of fighting ensue before the Puppet Master sees that their friendship is holding them back from genuinely hurting each other.&nbsp; In order to do the job properly, he'll have to take a <i>personal</i> hand in the battle, which will require his physical presence.&nbsp; He mentally orders Johnny to head toward Idlewild airport and prepares to head there himself.\n\nMeanwhile, back in Alicia's apartment, the blind sculptress puts two and two together and concludes that it was the Puppet Master who made Johnny act so oddly toward her.&nbsp; Her stepfather must still be <i>alive</i>!&nbsp; She jumps into action saying \"His last known hideout was near Idlewild Airport!\" and immediately heads off.\n\nAt the airport, it seems that all 4 of the characters arrive at once.&nbsp; The Puppet Master personally commands Johnny to fly directly toward a jet, and while Johnny has no choice but to obey, he's able to \"fly <i>through</i> the jet's innards and hurl himself harmlessly out again through the aircraft's exhaust!\"\n\nAlicia instructs Johnny to lower a flaming sheet of flame down toward the Puppet Master, which melts the villain's puppet and burns his hands.&nbsp; The Thing now realizes what happened, and all is well again, but the Puppet Master simply runs away, and no one bothers to chase after him (as he is unable to use his injured hands to mold more figurines).",
  "grade_one_liner": "A Puppet Master shortcut wrecks the plot, w/ Alicia improbably steering the chaos.\nThe rest is contradictions, contrivances, and a fight we see almost every month.",
  "subtitle": "Nothing New Under the Sun",
  "review_text": "In a word:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fonttype=italic; text=dreadful].&nbsp; Stan Lee must have decided to let a Thing/Torch battle take up a whole feature story instead of the normal couple of pages we see in just about <i>every</i> issue of the FF thus far.\n\n[ASSET: left | PuppetMasterIsBack | width=26%] The Puppet Master is <i>back</i> (and artist Dick Ayers at least does a good job depicting him with those villainous teeth).\n[BR]\n[BR] The last time readers saw him, he was in a submarine at the end of [LINK:1963:FF_14|FF #14|2026-08-06] (published eight months earlier), about to be destroyed by a giant octopus.&nbsp; So this story had to spend a page explaining how he survived.&nbsp; (And note this is the <i>second</i> time the Puppet Master has cheated what seemed like certain death.)\n\nJust like in [LINK:1963:FF_14|FF #14|2026-08-06], the Puppet Master doesn't actively engage with either the Human Torch or Thing.&nbsp; Rather, he serves only as the catalyst to stage the battle, in this case between Johnny and Ben.&nbsp; And he even drags his stepdaughter into the mix by mesmerizing Johnny into making a romantic gesture toward her, banking on the fact that when the Thing showed up moments later to see it, he would fly off the handle in rage.\n\n[ASSET: right | MissionAccomplished | width=17%] By the end of page three, you've pretty much seen the full extent of the Puppet Master's powers -- the ability to give Stan Lee a narrative shortcut to stage whatever battle happens to occur to him in the wee hours of the morning.\n[BR]\n[BR] (That, and the ability to blind people with those dazzling <i>teeth</i>!)\n\nKirby gave him menace.&nbsp; Ayers gives him full-on lighthouse-grade dental luminosity.\n[BR] At this point the man could probably signal aircraft without a flare gun!\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=But as a character, the Puppet Master is little more than a walking, talking plot device.]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And as a father figure, he seems to have no problem using his stepdaughter as fodder!)]\n\nIt's telling that every time the Puppet Master resurfaces, the story bends itself around whatever contrivance his powers make possible.&nbsp; He isn't a villain with motivations -- he's a narrative tool that lets Stan skip straight to the fireworks factory.&nbsp; And in this story, the shortcut is so blatant you can practically hear the gears grinding as the plot lurches into place.\n\n(And I'm upset that we <i>still</i> haven't been given any hint as to the source of his special clay.)\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut... say what you want about the threat level of the Puppet Master, you have to admit:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=his ploy works.]\n\n[ASSET: left | FlyingTelevision | width=18%] The battle between the Thing and Torch takes up most of the remaining page count, but on page 5, we see that the Thing has no problem smashing things in his girlfriend's apartment, tossing aside bookcases, televisions and the like.\n[BR]\n[BR] Which raises the question:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Why does a blind girl even <i>own</i> a television when a radio would do just as well, not to mention take up a lot less space?]\n\nCommon sense questions aside, the end result is the same:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Alicia's apartment is <i>ruined</i>, now with a big hole in the roof to <i>boot</i>!]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nBut the Puppet Master's plan to destroy the two friends from afar isn't good enough.&nbsp; Page 10 shows him realizing that the two combatants are holding back because of their friendship.&nbsp; And the only way to solve his problem is to get personally involved.\n[ASSET: center | FlawInThePlan | width=67%]\n\nThis he does by ordering Johnny to meet him at Idlewild airport, and he immediately takes a cab there <i>himself</i>.\n\n[ASSET: left | LastKnownHideout | width=22%] [ASSET: right | FlyingThruPlane | width=24%] Alicia's sudden guess that her stepfather would be at Idlewild Airport comes <i>totally</i> out of left field, and even though Johnny is mentally ordered to fly directly at an approaching airplane... he flies <i>through</i> it.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Stan, what have you been <i>smoking</i>?]\n\nThis is the kind of plotting that feels less \"Marvel Method\" and more \"Marvel Mad Libs,\" where the dice landed on jet engine and intangible fire boy and Stan just rolled with it.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Holy mother of mercy!)]&nbsp; It's chaos masquerading as choreography.\n\nIt's almost embarrassing that I have to point out that Alicia knew <i>nothing</i> of her stepfather's criminal tendencies when we first met her back in [LINK:1962:FF_8|FF #8|2026-04-30] (something I mentioned even <i>then</i> was highly unlikely), and after his apparent death at the end of that issue, she was as surprised as everyone else to learn of his return in [LINK:1963:FF_14|FF #14|2026-08-06], where he apparently dies <i>again</i>.\n\nYou can see for yourself in the left panel above that she's only <i>now</i> realizing that he's still alive!\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Suddenly she knows about his \"last known hideout?\"&nbsp; (<i>Give me a <b>break</b>!</i>)]\n\nAlicia's intuition here doesn't just come out of nowhere -- it <i>actively</i> contradicts the way she's been portrayed every single time her the Puppet Master appears.&nbsp; She's gone from \"tragically unaware of her stepfather's crimes\" to \"expert in his secret lairs\" in the span of a single panel.\n\nThe further suggestion that the Human Torch becomes intangible while he is in flaming form belies all the history we've seen, both here and in the pages of <i>Fantastic Four</i>.&nbsp; If Johnny truly does become ghostly when flamed on, then how is it that Ben has been able to punch him and Reed to grab him in the past (and even get burned in the process)?\n\nOne only needs to turn back to page 7 of this <i>very magazine</i> to see Ben grabbing Johnny in midair with the aid of an asbestos piece of air duct.&nbsp; Why didn't he pass harmlessly through the Torch like the spinning engine of the aircraft did on page 12?\n\nThe story can't even keep its own rules straight over the course of its thirteen pages.&nbsp; Johnny is solid when the plot needs him solid, intangible when the plot needs him intangible, and, I guess, combustible whenever Stan remembers he's supposed to be on fire.&nbsp; It's power‑set roulette, and every spin contradicts the last.\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; text=Talk about lazy <i>plotting</i>!&nbsp; Stan should be <i>ashamed</i> of himself!]\n\n[ASSET: left | WalkingFree | width=31%] Only Alicia's quick thinking on the last page (and the fact that the story had to quickly wrap up anyway) snaps Johnny out of his hypnotic spell and reveals to everyone that the Puppet Master was behind the whole thing.&nbsp; The villain's hands now burned, he is no longer able to carve figurines, and Alicia pleads with Ben that he be set free.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; text=And like complete <i>buffoons</i>, they just let the man stroll off into the sunset!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(<b><i>Unbelievable!</i></b>  Did the destruction of Alicia's apartment building mean <i>nothing?</i>)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nFrom an art perspective, Dick Ayers did an <i>adequate</i> job, even if not up to Jack Kirby-level standards.&nbsp; But my guess is that even \"King\" Kirby would not have been able to do much with this plot.&nbsp; Still, it's hard not to feel that Ayers' strengths lie more in inking than in carrying a full feature.\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nI'm not going to mince words:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=This was one of the <i>dumbest</i> Human Torch stories of <i>Strange Tales</i> so far.]\n\nNot only does the Puppet Master appear yet <i>again</i> (demonstrating how he seems to be able to cheat death faster than Stan Lee can dream up new plots), his plan to destroy the Fantastic Four by pitting two of its members against each other [SPAN: severity=warning; text=only gives readers more of what they already see on an almost-monthly basis], which is the Thing battling the Torch.\n\nThe author of the Biblical passage of <i>Ecclesiastes</i> 1:9 was sure right when he penned:\n\n[INDENT: left | 2rem] [SPAN: fonttype=mono; text=\"What has been will be again,]\n[BR] [INDENT: left | 4rem]&nbsp; [SPAN: fonttype=mono; text= what has been <i>done</i> will be done again];\n[BR] [INDENT: left | 4rem]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fonttype=mono; text= there is nothing new under the sun.\"]\n\nToss in the absolute wreckage the pair made of Alicia's apartment, the out-of-the-blue burst of intuition from the blind sculptress, and the head-scratching new power of the Human Torch not to get turned into spaghetti by a jet airplane's spinning engine, and you have a muddled mess of a plot that <i>strongly suggests</i> Marvel might have been better off missing their publication deadline this time around.\n\nThe splash page acknowledges that the story was \"Based upon an idea by Tommy and Jimmy Goodkind, Hewlett Harbor, New York.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; text=(Thanks for <i>nothing</i>, guys!)]\n\nI tell you:&nbsp; I'm no Doctor Strange fan, but thank <i>God</i> I get to turn the page to the next feature and put this absolute mess of a story behind me!&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold-italic; text=Bleah!]",
  "highlight": "The Human Torch and Thing clash again, dragged down by contrivances, contradictions, and a villain who just won't stay dead."
}